WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Water lets weak bonds add up to a fold.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.600
But ionic attractions need charges, and pH decides which groups carry them.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.120
Blood holds its pH in a window a tenth of a unit wide.

4
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.560
By the usual rule, its buffer is poor.

5
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:18.880
How does it hold?

6
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:23.873
Take a group that can hold a proton.

7
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:27.453
Below its pKa, it mostly keeps it.

8
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:30.033
Above, it mostly lets go.

9
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:32.633
At the pKa, a half and half.

10
00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:37.597
Each pH unit past the pKa shifts the ratio tenfold.

11
00:00:38.837 --> 00:00:42.197
One unit above, about 91% % have let go.

12
00:00:43.357 --> 00:00:46.217
Two units, about 99.

13
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:49.097
One pH unit is worth about one weak bond.

14
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:55.007
Near the pKa, both forms are plentiful, ready to absorb acid or base.

15
00:00:56.307 --> 00:00:58.687
Far away, one form has nearly run out.

16
00:00:59.787 --> 00:01:02.867
So a sealed flask buffers best within about one unit.

17
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.560
Blood's buffer is bicarbonate, with CO₂ as the acid side.

18
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.280
Its effective pKa is 6.1.

19
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.420
Blood sits at 7.4, 1.3 off.

20
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.140
About 20 bicarbonates per CO₂.

21
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:26.763
Take 24 mM bicarbonate and 1.2 mM CO₂.

22
00:01:28.103 --> 00:01:31.163
Seal the flask and add 10 mM strong acid.

23
00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:36.183
Each mM turns one bicarbonate into CO₂.

24
00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:39.223
Bicarbonate falls to 14.

25
00:01:40.523 --> 00:01:43.223
CO₂ piles up to 11.2.

26
00:01:44.443 --> 00:01:46.823
The ratio collapses to barely above one.

27
00:01:47.943 --> 00:01:50.163
The pH drops to about 6.2.

28
00:01:51.183 --> 00:01:52.683
Now open the flask.

29
00:01:54.063 --> 00:01:58.443
Breathing carries the extra CO₂ away, holding it at 1.2.

30
00:01:59.943 --> 00:02:03.363
Same acid. Where does the pH land now?

31
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.060
About 7.17. Bicarbonate falls to 14.

32
00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.620
CO₂ stays put, so the ratio stays near twelve to one.

33
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.760
The acid side never builds up.

34
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.920
That is the trick.

35
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.120
Blood's buffer works far from its pKa because blood is not sealed.

36
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.080
Breathing carries the extra CO₂ away.

37
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.010
Real blood does even better.

38
00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.190
Hemoglobin and other buffers take up part of the acid.

39
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.830
Later, the kidneys restore the bicarbonate.

40
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:50.600
Cross much past the window, and proteins change their charges and work less well.

41
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.240
The heart rhythm can falter.

42
00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:58.840
Less than one pH unit separates ordinary chemistry from emergency.

43
00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:02.913
A buffer's pKa is not the whole story.

44
00:03:04.273 --> 00:03:07.153
Blood's works because each breath throws out the acid side.

45
00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:10.613
That helps steady the charges weak bonds need.
